<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lament · 1851 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Old Folks at Home</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song of 1851 by Stephen Foster about longing for home by the Swanee River; it is the state song of Florida.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Stephen Foster</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Stephen Foster</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>American minstrel stage</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, United States</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1851</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words and music by Stephen Foster (d. 1864), written 1851)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-folks-at-home">Old Folks at Home</a>&quot;, also known as &quot;Swanee River&quot;, was commissioned from Stephen Foster in 1851 by E. P. Christy for his minstrel troupe, Christy's Minstrels. Christy also asked to be credited as its author and was named as such on early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printings</a> of the sheet music, so Foster gained little directly from its success, though he went on receiving royalties. Foster had written most of the words but could not settle on a river for the opening line. His brother Morrison later recalled that Stephen came into his office in Pittsburgh asking for a good two-syllable name for a Southern river; he turned down the Yazoo and the Pedee, and when the brothers looked at an atlas, Morrison's finger stopped at the Swanee, a small river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> flowing into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>. Foster never saw the Suwannee River or visited Florida, and few people outside Florida then knew it was a real place.</p><p>The song was written in the first person, in the voice and dialect of an enslaved African, at a time when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was legal in 15 of the states. Its narrator's longing for the old plantation has been criticised as romanticising slavery. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-e-b-du-bois">W. E. B. Du Bois</a>, in The Souls of Black Folk (1903), read the longing for the &quot;old folks at home&quot; otherwise: as a longing for the people and traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, where most of those enslaved in the New World had lived free before they were kidnapped and shipped across the Atlantic in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. In performance the dialect has long been replaced by standard American English, as at the 1955 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> Folk Festival, and the racial term in Foster's words has been replaced by others, such as &quot;brothers&quot;, &quot;children&quot; or &quot;dear ones&quot;.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonin-dvorak">Antonín Dvořák</a> arranged it for baritone, chorus and orchestra in 1894, and his Humoresque No. 7 of the 1890s is so like it that the two are sometimes played together, as Jean Arthur and Gary Cooper sang them in the film Mr. Deeds Goes to Town (1936). Early hit recordings ran from Len Spencer (1892) and the Haydn Quartet (1904) to Alma Gluck (1915), and in the 1930s it was revived by Jimmie Lunceford, Bunny Berigan and Bing Crosby. Ray Charles remade it as &quot;Swanee River Rock&quot; in 1957, and the Beach Boys turned it into &quot;South Bay Surfer&quot; in 1963.</p><p>In 1935 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> made it the state song, in place of &quot;Florida, My Florida&quot;, and it was traditionally sung at governors' inaugurations, with its words changed over time to give less offence: &quot;brothers&quot; was sung for the racial term at the dedication of the new state capitol in 1978. In 2007 Governor Charlie Crist left it out of his inauguration and used Charles Atkins's &quot;The Florida <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>&quot; instead, and a contest led by state senator Tony Hill, state representative Ed Homan and the Florida Music Education Association chose &quot;Florida (Where the Sawgrass Meets the Sky)&quot; on 11 January 2008. The legislature adopted that as the state anthem but kept &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-folks-at-home">Old Folks at Home</a>&quot; as the state song, with its original words replaced by a revised version approved by scholars at the Stephen Foster Memorial.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of homesickness: far from home, the singer longs for the Swanee River, the old plantation and the old folks he left there. Written in the voice of an enslaved man for the minstrel stage, it pictures the plantation as a lost happy home, which is why its words have been criticised and rewritten.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-caution" data-say>These are Foster's words as they were first sung and printed, in the mock dialect of the blackface minstrel stage, put in the mouth of an enslaved man who longs for the plantation. The chorus calls Black people by a racial slur of the time. They are printed as the historical record; Florida sang “brothers” in its place from 1978, and adopted revised words in 2008.</p><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Way down upon the Swanee ribber,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Far, far away,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Dere's wha my heart is turning ebber,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Dere's wha de old folks stay.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>All up and down de whole creation,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Sadly I roam;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Still longing for de old plantation,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And for de old folks at home.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>All de world am sad and dreary,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Ebry where I roam;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oh! darkeys, how my heart grows weary,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Far from de old folks at home.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>All round de little farm I wandered,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When I was young;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Den many happy days I squandered,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Many de songs I sung.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When I was playing wid my brudder,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Happy was I;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oh! take me to my kind old mudder,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Dere let me live and die.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>One little hut among de bushes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>One dat I love;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Still sadly to my memory rushes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>No matter where I rove.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When will I see de bees a humming,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>All round de comb?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When will I hear de banjo tumming,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Down in my good home,</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Stephen Foster (1851); a 19th-century song sheet, in its minstrel dialect. Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Old_Folks_at_Home_(song_sheet)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
